  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112900-the-template-was-not-unstable-it-was-stale"))
  (:adr/body "
## The item

The remaining layer-0 entry: oracle-store's last selector queried BOM in
the three ref-less repositories, an entity none of them declares.

## Why it was invisible

That selector asks whether a DIFFERENT entity holds none of the seeded
rows. It reaches the entity through the template's BOM, substituted to the
target's ref entity -- and a repository where no spec declares a ref has
no ref entity, so the token was dropped and the template's name stood.

It answered 0. The harness answers 0 too, because a real other entity
holds no rows. So the store layer measured five of five agreeing when it
landed, and the agreement was real -- but the two sides agreed for
different reasons, and this side was testing the weaker thing: that an
unknown entity is empty, rather than that the store partitions by entity.

## The fix is not visible in the value

Selector 7 was 0 and is 0. Selector 0 is 3, which is what shows the root
does hold rows, so the pair means something.

What changed is what the 0 MEANS. That is worth stating plainly because it
determines what can serve as evidence: not the number, which does not
move, but the entity name in the source and a check that refuses the old
form. A fix whose value does not move needs the check to come with it, or
there is nothing to see.

    com-aftership  BOM -> Checkpoint
    com-adyen      BOM -> Amount
    com-airtable   BOM -> Table

The replacement is the harness's own rule -- the first spec that is not
the root. Root was already derived identically on both sides (the first
spec with no refs), so the two now ask about the same entity rather than
two entities that merely both happen to be empty.

## The first floor could not see this, so there is a second

The stem check looks for the abbrobot stem and the two ref field names. A
template ENTITY that survives is none of those: BOM is not abbrobot. Every
check in the generator passed those three components.

The new floor refuses when a canon entity appears in a component that does
not declare it. Seen red by reverting only the fallback and keeping the
floor -- exactly the state that shipped -- which refuses and names BOM.

Its candidate set is the canon's three entities, and that is a recorded
limit rather than a silent one. Taking every capitalised quoted string out
of the template was tried first and is too broad: it flags GET, POST,
DELETE, TRUE and Yes, which are HTTP methods and parse fixtures. A fourth
template entity surviving would still pass, which is the same limit the
stem comment already describes.

## Two corrections to my own scan

A first pass compared each component's selector-7 entity against the first
spec that is not the first spec, and reported four repositories as
querying the wrong entity. That was wrong: the generator picks root as the
first spec with NO REFS, not the first spec. com-8th-wall's root is Device
-- the last of its six -- so the entity it queries is a correct non-root
one, and so are the other three. Only the three BOM cases were real.

The floor pattern was also wrong twice before it was right, and both times
it named things that are not entities. Recorded because the failure mode
is the same each time: a pattern broad enough to catch the case is broad
enough to catch much else, and the output looks like a finding.

## Controls

A ref-having repository is byte-identical under the patched generator and
prints no new line. The template remains a fixed point.
")))
